<http://data.silknow.org/object/635ef1f5-ae26-36eb-bd34-d5c8442f5de9>
        a                              ecrm:E22_Man-Made_Object ;
        rdfs:comment                   "Handkerchiefs made of plain linen served the same function in the 16th century as they do today. However, if they were decorated they could also be carried purely as fashionable accessories and given as gifts. Queen Elizabeth I frequently received sets of embroidered handkerchiefs on New Year's Day. The embroidery here, creating a pattern of stylised honeysuckle and grapevines, is partly worked in double running stitch. This is a double sided stitch, creating identical patterns on the front and back of the fabric. The metal lace adds to the showy effect, and its weight would have made the handkerchief drape gracefully. The initials 'E. M.' may indicate its maker, or its recipient, who could have been a man or a woman."@en , "Handkerchiefs made of plain linen served the same function in the sixteenth century as they do today.  However, if they were decorated they could also be carried purely as fashionable accessories and given as gifts.  Queen Elizabeth I frequently received sets of embroidered handkerchiefs on new Year's Day.  The embroidery here, creating a pattern of stylised honeysuckle and grapevines, is partly worked in double running stitch.  This is a double sided stitch, creating identical patterns on the front and back of the fabric.  The metal lace adds to the showy effect, and its weight would have made the handkerchief drape gracefully.  The initials 'em' may indicate its maker, or its recipient (male or female) as a gift."@en , "Man's or woman's linen handkerchief, c1600, English; embroidered with crimson silk, silve- gilt bobbin lace trim"@en , "Handkerchief of fine bleached linen (approx 50 threads per cm, balanced plain weave) with a 1⅞-inch (4.7 cm) border, embroidered with crimson silk floss.  The embroidery is reversible, worked in counted thread, double running stitch and detached buttonhole stitches.  It is trimmed with ½-inch wide (1.3 cm) silver-gilt bobbin lace."@en ;
        ecrm:P138i_has_representation  <http://data.silknow.org/image/b272abae-cc45-3933-a17d-8c5eb9315a7e> , <http://data.silknow.org/image/1664f795-0a62-365b-b995-3269ee6ccce2> , <http://data.silknow.org/image/3da6cfca-8358-3400-9622-85abee568586> , <http://data.silknow.org/image/b6e52ef4-061b-31d9-a610-e9738e2272ce> , <http://data.silknow.org/image/573bbe77-c4c9-3c66-8105-5e2894474de7> , <http://data.silknow.org/image/50d61ead-8df4-3a54-bcc3-bbff81ff526a> ;
        ecrm:P3_has_note               "Man's or woman's linen handkerchief, c1600, English; embroidered with crimson silk, silve- gilt bobbin lace trim"@en , "Handkerchief of fine bleached linen (approx 50 threads per cm, balanced plain weave) with a 1⅞-inch (4.7 cm) border, embroidered with crimson silk floss.  The embroidery is reversible, worked in counted thread, double running stitch and detached buttonhole stitches.  It is trimmed with ½-inch wide (1.3 cm) silver-gilt bobbin lace."@en , "Handkerchiefs made of plain linen served the same function in the 16th century as they do today. However, if they were decorated they could also be carried purely as fashionable accessories and given as gifts. Queen Elizabeth I frequently received sets of embroidered handkerchiefs on New Year's Day. The embroidery here, creating a pattern of stylised honeysuckle and grapevines, is partly worked in double running stitch. This is a double sided stitch, creating identical patterns on the front and back of the fabric. The metal lace adds to the showy effect, and its weight would have made the handkerchief drape gracefully. The initials 'E. M.' may indicate its maker, or its recipient, who could have been a man or a woman."@en , "Handkerchiefs made of plain linen served the same function in the sixteenth century as they do today.  However, if they were decorated they could also be carried purely as fashionable accessories and given as gifts.  Queen Elizabeth I frequently received sets of embroidered handkerchiefs on new Year's Day.  The embroidery here, creating a pattern of stylised honeysuckle and grapevines, is partly worked in double running stitch.  This is a double sided stitch, creating identical patterns on the front and back of the fabric.  The metal lace adds to the showy effect, and its weight would have made the handkerchief drape gracefully.  The initials 'em' may indicate its maker, or its recipient (male or female) as a gift."@en ;
        ecrm:P43_has_dimension         <http://data.silknow.org/object/635ef1f5-ae26-36eb-bd34-d5c8442f5de9/dimension/2> , <http://data.silknow.org/object/635ef1f5-ae26-36eb-bd34-d5c8442f5de9/dimension/1> ;
